# Tiled grid evaluation of LC questions · `spec:nrz:lc-grid-tiles`

**Principle (LC evaluation is two graphs joined by bindings)** · `prin:nrz:lc-grid-contract`

An LC grid evaluation is a descriptor graph lowered to a computational graph through checked bindings under (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-two-graphs`), (`spec:nrz:descriptor-heap-computation`). Exact relation, domain, cover and evidence nodes are shared at tile scope; varying inputs and outputs are indexed symbols bound to native float region versions. Kernel applications connect those versions, and decision nodes provide the sole numerical-to-descriptor return path (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-certified-feedback`). Every cell's own enclosed answer is a selection of its output binding. Reuse follows graph identity and dependencies under (`spec:nrz:reuse-concept`). The open requirements below delimit capabilities that existing LC covers do not establish.

## Measured standing · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-standing`

**Observation (Measurement conditions)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-measurement-conditions`

A throwaway cost measurement against main revision `ea2a94f`, with measured paths unchanged at source revision `bc1ad28388d2e9ebe268ff01f46ec9e19624b83b`, uses a release build, `f64`, one core of a Ryzen 9 5950X and one run per size. Work is the budget's exact count, distinct from elapsed time. The forward question varies L at every cell, fixes C to `1/4`, requests one time point and has cycle range `[0,0]`; the selected inputs make the L*C endpoint products rational squares. The supplied measurement evidence identifies no measurement date or repeated-run uncertainty, so neither is inferred.

**Observation (Rebuilding the forward question at every cell)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-forward-cost`

For grid populations of 10, 100, 1,000 and 10,000 cells, answering from scratch costs respectively 237, 256, 278 and 270 microseconds per cell, and 74,496, 79,071, 83,698 and 88,691 Work per cell. Growing exact fractions account for the increasing Work; peak memory stays at 4.6 MB. `LcRelation::forward_cover` accounts for about 85 percent of elapsed time, rebuilding the exact cover at each cell; `LcRelation::new` and `LcForwardProjection::new` account for about four percent each. Extrapolating 270 microseconds to a billion cells gives about 75 core-hours; this is an extrapolation, not a measured billion-cell run. The supported forward contract is (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`).

**Observation (Whole-box and prepared-family controls)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-reuse-controls`

Answering one box over the whole L range costs 231 microseconds and 70,369 Work independently of the number of cells represented, but returns one wide enclosure. Reading members from one prepared zero-time cover through `LcTimeProjection::member` costs 7.3 to 8.9 microseconds and 2,213 to 2,800 Work per member. One `LcTimeProjection::tail` call encloses all members from index 15 onward in four microseconds. These controls demonstrate useful family reuse under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-zero-time-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`); a wide common enclosure supplies no cell-specific answer, and the measured member route still spends Work after preparation.

**Observation (Rational-square admission defect)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-square-refusal`

`LcRelation::forward_cover` returns `LcRelationError::Projection` when either extremal L*C product lacks a rational square root. Its private `inverse_root` in `crates/bindings/src/lc_relation/forward/rectangle.rs` constructs the exact positive root, then requires `as_rational()` before taking its reciprocal. A generic positive L by C grid is therefore refused almost everywhere by this adapter, despite the mathematical frequency being defined. The rational extremal-rate restriction is explicit in (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`). This domain restriction and the repeated exact work of (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-forward-cost`) are separate defects: speeding up the square-only path does not supply general positive LC evaluation.

## Tile contract · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-tile-contract`

**Definition (Grid axis and indexed inputs)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-inputs`

A grid is an exact finite axis descriptor, or a finite Cartesian product of ordered axis descriptors, in the descriptor graph under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-grid-symbol`). Its domain indexes instances of the original LC relation (`def:nrz:lc-relation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-any-direction`). Each varying input is an indexed symbol over that domain; its active tile restriction is bound to one native region, with companion symbols bound to their regions. The global axis domain does not require simultaneous materialization of all its numerical selections. Axes may vary L, C, initial phase, query time, phase-time observations, amplitudes, energy or deterministic supports. Supplied and queried roles are edges to the original relation node, not extra inferred constraints. A series adds a sample-axis descriptor and a shared epoch node. Shared uncertainty sources remain shared nodes with their original joint-domain edges under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-source-symbols`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`); an axis product does not prove independence. Statistical moments alone supply no bounded-support edge (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-certified-sets`).

**Definition (Tile subdomain and inclusion)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-tile`

A tile is a sub-box of the grid's axis ranges, represented by a subdomain node with a checked inclusion edge to its parent. Its physical input-box node encloses every admitted input support selected by that subdomain, including the retained joint constraints. The domain and box are related by the original indexed input bindings; a point or uncertain support is selected through those bindings. The tile fixes the question schema, supplied/query roles, sample axes, meanings and original source-map family. Its certificate applies throughout the physical box, including admissible values between sampled coordinates. Domain-inclusion and boundary-ownership edges assign every grid index to one tile even when numerical support boxes overlap. Different source or schema families require separately admitted domain nodes. A tile partitions execution of the original relation; it does not replace that relation or derive existence from sampling.

**Rule (Shared exact certificate edges)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-tile-certificate`

Tile preparation establishes evidence edges from the tile domain to the original relation, complete projection cover, cycle-domain, branch-boundary, physical-admission and pi nodes. Each fact has explicit proven, assumed or open standing: physical input assumptions stay assumptions, checked cover/cut facts carry their proofs, and unresolved obligations remain open. A finalized certified tile has all evidence required by its requested conclusion. Checked existence or emptiness and structural verdicts require their own edges; an enclosure alone supplies none of them. Parameterized output and companion symbols and their lowering relations are prepared at the same scope. The certificate quantifies over the varying input coordinates under (`def:nrz:reuse-lambda`), rather than extending a ground point proof by assertion. Its nodes are shared by all indices in the tile. For a fixed box, schema, proof precision and branch complexity, exact preparation is independent of cell population. Uniform branch facts and complete alternative-family nodes retain their distinct meanings under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`).

**Rule (Shared nodes and hoisted numerical values)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-shared-nodes`

The original relation, exact pi/full-turn definitions, root-bracket proofs and admitted cover/branch schemas are shared nodes across every tile whose domain and dependency edges permit reuse. A child's inclusion proof transports valid parent evidence; tighter cuts add new evidence without copying or rebuilding the parent graph. Exact nodes independent of the grid index lower to a shared numerical prelude under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-hoisted-constants`), evaluated once per compatible node-and-version key in (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-reuse-key`). Shared symbols may have several checked region bindings; equal float values alone create no sharing relation. A changed guard or source version prevents numerical reuse even if the same symbolic node survives.

**Rule (Indexed native bindings with checked overlap)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-array-binding`

All continuously varying values, deterministic bounds and numerical errors are native float arrays bound to indexed tile symbols under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-grid-symbol`), (`spec:nrz:descriptor-binding`). Bindings connect symbols and region-version selections many-to-many; every overlap requires a checked reading relation under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-checked-overlap`), and every heap entry has binding coverage under (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-total`). Original source, kind, unit, axis and map dependencies are checked once as a parameterized family. Native containment guards check each selected complete support against the tile's certified box, including conversion error. Cycle indices remain exact descriptor-domain parameters outside the floating heap. Array entries cause no new exact rational literals, scalar descriptor nodes or exact admission edges. Live version and containment checks remain numerical work. The required family admission is open under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-binding`).

**Rule (Output symbols and bindings precede execution)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-output-binding`

Each queried quantity is an output symbol in the descriptor graph indexed over the tile axis, with sample or exact cycle axes where its family requires them. Lowering derives its sort, kind, unit, domains and companion-symbol relations and binds it to a reserved indexed result region before any kernel runs, following (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-result-symbols`), (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-companion-symbols`). Total-bound and numerical-error symbols bind to their own companion regions with checked target edges. Pending bindings become readable only on complete publication. Afterwards the result is resolved by output symbol and selected binding to the live region version under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-validation`); selecting an axis position uses that admitted binding without constructing another scalar descriptor. Family membership, branch association and feasibility remain evidence edges on the output/domain graph, not facts inferred from its numerical centers.

**Rule (Heap pass as a lowered subgraph)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-heap-pass`

One tile pass executes the lowered computation subgraph of (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-lowered-subgraph`): a retained kernel schedule whose application edges map indexed input-region versions and shared prelude versions to indexed output-region and companion versions. Its bounded numerical stages evaluate all selected entries without symbolic evaluation per cell. Each output selection has its own freshly computed enclosure or family of enclosures from that selection's actual heap inputs, with total deterministic coverage and separately tracked numerical error. Import, arithmetic and restoration error occur once under (`thm:nrz:descriptor-binding-kernel-enclosure`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-error-composition`). No remembered value, interpolation or copied wide tile hull supplies the answer. The shared parameterized lowering and authenticated input containment justify the output binding without adding exact nodes or edges for each cell.

**Rule (Node identity and version-dependent authority)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-lifetime`

Reuse is identified by descriptor node identity plus the bound input-region versions, extended by the complete domain, context, source/map, companion, representation, arithmetic and proof dependencies in (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-reuse-key`). The tile domain's inclusion, cover and boundary nodes belong to that dependency closure. A new input or companion version invalidates dependent output bindings and decision strata through the edges of (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-invalidation`), (`rule:nrz:decision-invalidation`), even when center bytes agree. An immutable relation or certificate node survives when its premises still apply; it does not authenticate the new numerical version. Rebinding within that same family can reuse its exact authority after native validation. Widened supports or changed premises need new admission edges. Output and companion bindings publish atomically under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-atomic-publication`); selected strata publish with their checked evidence under (`rule:nrz:decision-selected-publication`). Failed publication leaves the affected output symbols unavailable, and archived theorem nodes retain historical authority under (`rule:nrz:decision-attachment-lifetime`).

**Rule (Splits add domains and evidence edges)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`

When one tile cannot certify its required cycle range, branch or threshold conclusion across its whole box, refine its enclosure evidence or add child subdomain nodes. Checked inclusion edges connect each child to the parent, a checked cover edge proves that their union covers the parent, and boundary-ownership edges connect them to shared cycle-face, branch-cut or threshold nodes. Children reuse the parent's relation, admission and valid proof nodes through these edges; they never rebuild that authority. A tighter bracket or newly settled cut adds only its missing nodes and proof edges. This is the region-containment refinement of (`spec:nrz:lattice-decisions`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-boundary-selection`) and (`rule:nrz:decision-refinement`) in `docs/design/decision-nodes.md`. Settled strata retain their complete evidence; unresolved subdomains receive the extra exact work. Conservative enclosures can cause proof-induced splits even where the true answer is constant; measure that graph growth separately. Changing numerical values within an already certified branch does not add exact structure per cell.

**Rule (Termination and undecided strata)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-stop`

A declared refinement-policy node bounds cumulative exact Work, numerical work, bytes, depth and attempts, and identifies allowed formats and deterministic error goals. Each split reduces an index subdomain or tightens an admitted physical subdomain and consumes the finite allowance. An uncertain support at a singleton grid index can still straddle a boundary; physical child domains retain inclusion and cover edges to that same support, not new independent sources. Increasing precision cannot remove physical uncertainty. Refinement stops at complete evidence or the applicable limit. An undecided stratum retains open obligation edges, its last valid enclosure as a bound region version, and a typed stop node for `BoundaryIntersection`, `UnsupportedCertificate`, `RefinementExhausted` or `DecisionBudgetExhausted` under (`def:nrz:decision-outcomes`). If no finite enclosure is justified, its complete candidate-domain node remains explicit. Such a stratum asserts no complement membership or guessed branch (`rule:nrz:decision-undecided-continuation`). Invalid meanings, stale dependencies, false evidence and representation failures have typed refusal nodes and unavailable output bindings, separately from mathematical undecided or impossible outcomes.

**Definition (Cost as graph growth and numerical evaluation)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-cost`

Let N be the grid population, T the finalized tile count, R the number of additional certificate attempts and refinements, and B the maximum resident cell population. Exact Work charges the nodes and edges constructed, checked, compared or reused during the T setups and R refinements; count new descriptor/authority nodes and evidence edges alongside actual CAS Work, including failed attempts. Bit size, branch-domain complexity and proof precision remain explicit cost dimensions. After finalized tile setup, the heap pass adds zero exact nodes or edges, performs zero CAS invocations and constructs zero descriptors per cell. Its graph growth consists of native region versions and kernel-application edges for the bounded indexed schedule, not a kernel graph cloned at each cell. Arithmetic, native containment and version checks remain per-cell time. For fixed samples and output/branch arity, time is linear in evaluated cells and heap storage is proportional to B plus bounded scratch and shared prelude regions. Series length and materialized cycle extent are separate dimensions. Boundary singleton tiles may add exact structure; making every interior cell a singleton fails the uniform-domain sharing criterion.

**Rule (Bounded live subgraphs and retained evidence)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-streaming`

A bounded tile schedule reuses numerical buffers as it advances over axis subdomains. Inputs and complete output bindings can be streamed; the live computational subgraph, scratch, referenced authority and pending refinement frontier have declared bounds independent of N. Immutable evidence can remain in a retained or exported authority graph with separately measured storage; a consumer retaining all indexed outputs pays their explicit output-size cost. Retiring numerical regions preserves resolvable symbol-to-evidence paths without retaining every prior live version. Region decisions evaluate covering-box bindings directly, without allocating a region entry for every interior cell. This graph and binding lifetime contract is open under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-bounded-execution`).

**Rule (Tile size changes cost within one question graph)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`

The extent of a tile domain is bounded by validity of its evidence edges, heap and scratch capacity, output-family extent and enclosure-error growth. Fixed extents, adaptive subdivision or adaptation under a capacity cap use the same domain and cut proofs. Smaller domain nodes can reduce overestimation while adding exact setup structure; larger domains amortize that structure only while their premises and requested error goal hold. The choice changes cost, never the original question graph, mathematical answer, branch coverage or boundary convention. Output enclosures may differ within the same tracked-error contract, and exhausted allowances can leave an undecided stratum instead of a settled one, never incompatible certified facts (`thm:nrz:decision-reproducibility`). Tile selection cannot relax input uncertainty or tolerance. The exposed policy remains (`open:nrz:lc-grid-size-policy`).

## Exact frequency without rational roots · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-frequency`

**Rule (Positive-root expression and its numerical binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-exact-frequency`

For L>0 and C>0 the descriptor graph retains the exact operation `omega0=(1 rad)/sqrt(L*C)`, with operand edges to the indexed L/C symbols and positivity, root and reciprocal-domain evidence under (`def:nrz:lc-relation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-kinds`). Its coherent-rate node is `a=omega0/(1 rad)`. Rational inputs can give an exact algebraic root; varying inputs use the same expression over the admitted parameter domain. Rational normalization is optional. Before execution, the frequency and error-companion symbols bind to indexed result regions; the heap evaluates product, positive root and reciprocal with outward error propagation. A numerical root never replaces the exact operation node, and a rational bracket is an enclosure-evidence node for that expression, not a surrogate expression. Rational squares remain simplifications, never admission preconditions.

**Rule (Certified rational brackets settle cover cuts)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-root-brackets`

For an independent positive box with rational coherent product bounds `0<p_min<=L*C<=p_max`, exact arithmetic can check positive rationals r and s satisfying `r*r<=p_min` and `p_max<=s*s`. Thus `sqrt(L*C)` lies in `[r,s]` and a in `[1/s,1/r]`; tighter endpoint brackets can be certified by the same squared inequalities. For correlated inputs these product bounds are outer coverage under the retained joint premises. Combine such rational bounds with independently checked pi bounds to enclose the original lifted phase divided by `2*pi rad`. Exact rational comparisons then certify endpoint cycle indices and completeness of the intervening range using the original floor convention (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-cycle-branches`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-affine-enclosure`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-floor-selection`). Signed time and correlated phase inputs need their own whole-box extrema under the same original constraints. Bracket overlap with a cut triggers refinement, a split or undecided; equality at an irrational face needs an exact identity or other checked equality proof, not a tolerance. Bracketing permits conservative complete covers without falsely claiming that every enclosed endpoint or branch is attained.

**Rule (Frequency proof and execution edges retain their owners)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-frequency-owners`

CAS checks positive-root, squared-bracket, pi, complete cycle-domain and cut evidence at tile scope; these are exact nodes and proof edges shared by compatible domains. Bindings checks original physical correspondence, quantity kinds, source maps and the relation between indexed symbols and lowered numerical laws. Scalar supplies product, root, reciprocal and pi enclosure laws; matrix validates native input/companion versions and coverage and executes their kernel edges. Feasibility, emptiness, overlap-tail and horizon facts need their own evidence edges from the original domain, not merely a nonempty bracket (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-horizon`). Generic irrational-root cover edges and their parameterized numerical lowering remain open under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-irrational-cover`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`).

## LC questions and decisions · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-questions`

**Rule (Time to phase series binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-forward-series`

For supplied L, C, initial phase and a time-series symbol, the tile's evidence edges certify the positive or quiescent domain, complete lifted-phase/cycle cover, timing correlations and applicable forward-horizon derivation. The output is a phase symbol indexed over the product of the tile axis and a second, sample axis, with branch-family structure where wrapping requires multiple arcs. Lowering binds that symbol and its deterministic-bound and numerical-error companions to indexed result regions before kernels run. The lowered subgraph shares its frequency evaluation across the sample axis where dependencies permit, then computes each selected phase enclosure from its own inputs with exact wrap-boundary ownership. A requested horizon is a separate output symbol with finite, infinite or open-domain evidence. The sample-axis and shared-clock nodes remain attached under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-time-queries`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-horizon`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`); the independent fixture is (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-forward-input-boxes`). Changes of cycle or whole-circle regime refine the domain through (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`).

**Rule (Inductance output and alias-domain binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-inductance-query`

For a query in L, the tile certifies projection of the original observation and supplied-constraint graph, complete rate/product alias domains, positive divisors and remaining scale directions. The output L symbol is indexed over the tile axis and, when necessary, the retained alias-family domain, with total-bound and numerical-error symbols bound before execution to corresponding result regions. Shared product P and rate nodes retain their relation to the original observations. Supplied positive C permits `L=P/C`; positive amplitude ratio R permits `L=R*sqrt(P)`; energy and positive current amplitude permit `L=2*E/Ihat^2`. Checked lowering connects those operations to a heap subgraph using each input selection, preserving every other supplied constraint and error companion. The precise meanings and fixtures are (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-any-direction`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-known-element`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-amplitude-energy`). Energy alone leaves a free-scale node and its evidence under (`arg:nrz:lc-relation-energy-identifiability`), rather than an invented bounded L binding.

**Rule (Capacitance output and common-product binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-capacitance-query`

For a query in C, the tile certifies the original joint observation/alias graph and elimination domains. The output C symbol is indexed over the tile and retained alias axes, bound at lowering with its total-bound and numerical-error companions. Supplied positive L permits `C=P/L`; positive amplitude ratio R permits `C=sqrt(P)/R`; energy and positive voltage amplitude permit `C=2*E/Vhat^2`. Their kernel edges compute the indexed C enclosure regions from actual input versions. Simultaneous L and C outputs share the same product/rate and source nodes; two marginal bindings introduce no independence edge (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-known-element`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-amplitude-energy`). The restricted capacitor-interval route of (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-capacitance-implementation`) is evidence for its own graph, not a generic family lowering or a universal determinacy theorem.

**Rule (Phase to indexed time-family binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-inverse-time`

For a target-phase symbol and epoch node, the tile certifies the original joint phase/rate relation, complete cycle-index domain, ordering or ambiguous-numbering facts and any justified overlap tail. The output is an indexed time-family node over the tile axis and exact cycle domain. A member is a projection node of that family, and a tail is a subfamily node with its covering or existence evidence and a start-time output symbol. Their schemas and requested numerical selections bind before execution to result and error-companion regions; the heap pass does not construct one exact member node per cell. On an admitted branch, kernel edges enclose `t_n=(theta_target-theta0+2*pi*n rad)/omega0`. The independent zero-phase rectangle specializes to `I_n=[2*pi*n/a_max,2*pi*n/a_min]` under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-time-queries`); correlated inferred phase/rate retains its shared constraint nodes. Tail status and exact-frequency exceptions follow (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`), (`conv:nrz:lc-relation-tail-status`). Finite numerical selections cannot truncate an unbounded family domain. `LcTimeProjection` supplies existing member/tail semantics; indexed numerical lowering without residual per-member exact work is open under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`).

**Rule (Projection verdicts are scoped evidence facts)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-projection-verdicts`

Structural determinacy and numerical certainty are separate facts attached to projection-domain or stratum nodes under (`def:nrz:lc-relation-determinacy`), (`def:nrz:lc-relation-certainty`). A tile cover has no per-index feasibility consequence without a parameterized witness or existence edge scoped to that input family. Alias-family nodes retain their original-constraint and verdict dependencies. Positive-amplitude and quiescent domain nodes obey (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-kinds`): at rest the frequency binding can remain defined while physical oscillation phase has a domain-outcome node. Certified empty, underdetermined, defined-domain, undecided and refused strata have distinct evidence and availability consequences. Tightened supports and added observations preserve information by inclusion and proof edges under (`req:nrz:lc-relation-refinement-monotonicity`), including open obligations after exhausted refinement.

**Definition (Decision predicate and boundary nodes)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-predicate`

A decision node declares its exact predicate, threshold symbol, units, quantifier and domain before evaluation. Its partition node is connected to shared threshold, cycle-face and branch-cut boundary nodes with explicit strict/inclusive ownership under (`def:nrz:decision-partition`), (`rule:nrz:decision-cut-predicates`). The node consumes authenticated output-enclosure bindings and the exact partition; a kernel comparison alone cannot add a semantic fact. For a universal predicate over a feasible input family, yes requires evidence covering all values, and no requires a counterexample or opposite-side coverage together with nonemptiness. For an existential predicate, yes requires existence and no requires complete exclusion. A singleton is a special domain, not a different decision mechanism. Empty physical domains have explicit outcome nodes rather than vacuous physical assertions. Every conclusion retains separate enclosure and cut or witness evidence under (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`).

**Rule (Decision strata over tile indices)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-cell-decisions`

A tile decision node partitions its index domain into stratum nodes with checked cover and disjointness edges. Certified-yes and certified-no strata carry descriptor-layer facts with the corresponding enclosure/cut or witness edges; an undecided stratum carries open obligations, a bound last-enclosure version and a typed stop node under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-stop`). These are graph strata, not scalar truth values or a mathematical third region complement (`rule:nrz:decision-undecided-continuation`). A uniform tile conclusion yields one shared fact over its whole stratum. Nonuniform conclusions require child-domain refinement, with new exact structure charged there and no CAS replay per numerical entry. Selection of an individual index resolves the stratum's fact and its indexed output binding, without adding a fact node for that cell. Refusal subdomains attach typed failure nodes and unavailable bindings and remain covered. Later continuations depend on the selected stratum through (`rule:nrz:decision-stratification`), (`rule:nrz:decision-stratum-sharing`); they cannot justify the enclosure that selected them. This parameterized decision capability remains (`req:nrz:lc-grid-region-evidence`).

**Rule (Region decision graph and covering evidence)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-region-decisions`

A region decision is a decision node over a declared domain node: a grid-index domain or a continuous physical region. Its predicate includes the existential or universal quantifier. Bound covering-box enclosures feed the decision, whose answer is a stratum partition of that domain with checked coverage and branch/verdict evidence. Complete yes coverage proves a universal yes; a checked counterexample proves its no. A checked witness proves an existential yes; complete no coverage proves its no. Open strata retain unresolved boxes and obligations without forcing an aggregate verdict. Settled interior strata need no cell enumeration; only threshold-straddling or otherwise unresolved boundary evidence receives refinement. A finite grid partition has no continuous-region cover edge merely because all samples decide (`rule:nrz:decision-membership-locality`), (`rule:nrz:decision-batch-strata`). If point outputs are requested, an output symbol binds over the strata's index ranges to result and companion regions, with explicit linear materialization cost. Existing decision/lattice containment rules supply the proof standard under (`rule:nrz:decision-refinement`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-boundary-selection`); the region scheduler and cover graph remain open.

## Acceptance measurement · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-acceptance`

**Requirement (Rerun the measured workload over tile graphs)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-benchmark`

Rerun (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-measurement-conditions`) through tile-domain admission and the lowered heap subgraph at populations 10, 100, 1,000 and successive powers of ten through 100,000,000. Cover every question kind in (`sec:nrz:lc-grid-questions`), both decision forms, rational-square controls and generic positive non-square L*C inputs. Retain the release `f64`, single-core workload for comparability and identify actual hardware, toolchain, arithmetic revisions and policies. Use repeated independent timings with raw observations, warm/cold setup state and a declared interval-estimation method; the one-run baseline supplies no retrospective intervals. Include domains sharing one certificate, tile-capacity crossings, cycle/branch/threshold boundaries, exact equality, correlated supports, quiescent domains, aliases and exhausted-refinement strata. Hold sample-axis extent and numerical branch/output extent fixed for primary cell-scaling fits, with separate controls varying those dimensions. All measurement outputs belong to acceptance evidence; they are not the representation of LC results.

**Requirement (Setup, exponent and exact graph attribution)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`

For each question kind and fixed workload/policy, fit total time `t(N)=k+c*N^p` and report setup cost k, coefficient c, exponent p and their uncertainty intervals, units, confidence level, residuals and raw timings. Measure setup directly and steady per-cell heap time; c is a per-cell cost only when p is one. Report tile-domain count, extents, capacity, new and shared exact nodes/edges, refinement count, measured setup-time sum and per-tile exact Work distributions. Separate preparation and already-prepared-subgraph fits distinguish proof sharing from a small coefficient hiding repeated exact graph construction. If tile count grows with N, its setup contributes to the curve and cannot all be assigned to a constant intercept k; expose that contribution and distinct capacity regimes. Reject linearity claims based on intervals too broad to distinguish linear from superlinear growth.

**Requirement (Graph counters, memory and exhaustive domain coverage)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-counters`

Count exact nodes and edges added, CAS invocations, descriptor constructions and exact Work at tile setup and each refinement, including failed attempts. All are zero per cell after finalized setup. Count separately the native region versions and kernel-application edges added by the indexed heap pass, plus native arithmetic work. Report heap bytes per resident cell, scratch, live authority references, absolute peak and exported graph/output storage while N increases at a fixed capacity. Classify the index domain by completed, undecided and refused strata, with a checked disjoint cover edge establishing exhaustive, once-only accounting; no independent result census supplies that proof. Measure each stratum's cardinality by reason, including non-square input, unsupported certificate, boundary, exhausted allowance, numerical range, invalid domain/meaning and stale evidence. Rational-square and boundary-domain predicates remain independent of outcome classification. Failed and open subdomains remain in the timing denominator. Complete shared authority, companion versions and scheduling storage must be included so streaming does not conceal retained graph growth.

**Requirement (Decision strata and boxes against boundary share)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`

For region decisions, measure covering-box nodes evaluated, settled stratum nodes, unresolved stratum nodes, child-domain depth, exact nodes/edges and Work against both N and the count/share of cells intersecting a declared neighborhood of the true decision boundary. Define that neighborhood from physical support, requested resolution and certified error, not observed failures. Vary resolution at fixed geometry, interior volume at fixed boundary resolution and boundary complexity, including wholly settled domains and exact faces. Separate region-graph evaluation from optional indexed output materialization and streaming input cost. Expected growth follows covering evidence and unresolved boundary strata, not exact nodes or kernel invocations for every interior cell. If all physical supports straddle the boundary, its share can be one; expose that worst case rather than promising sublinear evaluation for every domain.

**Criterion (Structural acceptance is pass or fail)** · `crit:nrz:lc-grid-structural-acceptance`

Acceptance requires zero exact nodes/edges, CAS calls, descriptor constructions and exact Work per cell after tile setup; no working-heap growth with total grid size at fixed capacity; and p equal to one within a sufficiently informative reported interval for full indexed output evaluation at fixed output complexity. Region-only decision growth must follow boundary-sensitive covering boxes and strata under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`), with settled interiors discharged without cell enumeration. Uniform-box controls must show population-independent exact certificate graphs and Work; renaming an exact per-cell graph as singleton tiles fails. Checked stratum covers must retain every index and honest outcome, and generic positive non-square inputs cannot be refused merely for irrationality. Independent oracles and mutation/boundary controls verify semantics alongside graph counters. Region classification need not have a linear exponent; optional output materialization has its separately reported linear cost. These criteria apply to a future implementation and are not measured results of this concept.

**Criterion (Uncertified ratio is a non-blocking target)** · `crit:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-target`

Measure plain uncertified `f64` evaluation of the same closed form, inputs, output-axis extent, branch convention, hardware and scheduling conditions. Report absolute per-cell time and the certified steady per-cell cost divided by that baseline, with intervals; distinguish exact graph setup, decision refinement and numerical output materialization. Prevent dead-code elimination and keep array traversal comparable. The owner chooses the multiple under (`open:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-policy`); this concept establishes none. The target is non-blocking and cannot weaken coverage, exclude unresolved strata from costs or replace (`crit:nrz:lc-grid-structural-acceptance`).

## Existing structures and open capabilities · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-engine`

**Rule (Existing covers become shared graph authority)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-existing-covers`

`LcRelation`, `LcForwardCover`, `LcForwardProjection` and pair/scale projection owners provide existing relation, cover and branch authority under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-pairs-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-scale-implementation`). The tile graph references that authority under its original premises rather than duplicating it per index. `LcTimeProjection` supplies complete member/tail semantics and existence strength under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-zero-time-implementation`). Existing owners do not yet supply arbitrary indexed live bindings, parameterized tile-domain evidence or zero exact graph growth per member; (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-reuse-controls`) measures the remaining Work. Family nodes with shared member/tail projection schemas, rather than exact construction at every numerical selection, are the open requirement.

**Rule (Existing execution contracts and graph boundaries)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-existing-execution`

Indexed grid symbols, predeclared result/companion symbols, checked overlap, hoisted constants and the lowered computation subgraph carry over from (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-grid-symbol`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-result-symbols`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-checked-overlap`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-hoisted-constants`), (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-lowered-subgraph`). Plans and batches contribute dependency, source and bounded-execution contracts under (`def:nrz:computation-plan`), (`rule:nrz:plan-resource-ownership`), (`rule:nrz:batch-variation`); their grid realization here is the indexed graph binding. LC spectral batches supply existing shared-schema/prelude precedents (`rule:nrz:lc-resonance-plan-strata`), (`rule:nrz:lc-plan-bindings`), (`obs:nrz:lc-plan-spectral-executor`). Scalar laws, matrix version nodes, kernel edges and shared `PiConstant` supply numerical ingredients, while decision nodes retain exact enclosure/cut proofs and strata (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`), (`rule:nrz:decision-stratum-sharing`). None of these contracts alone implements arbitrary LC tile projection or region refinement.

**Requirement (Parameterized tile authority graph)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-tile-authority`

Open capability, owned by CAS for mathematical authority and bindings for LC correspondence: admit tile-domain and physical-box nodes with inclusion, relation, complete-cover, cycle-domain, boundary and admission evidence edges, quantified over varying parameters. Share the original relation, pi, bracket and valid ancestor authority across compatible domains; add only missing refinement proofs. A point proof or schema-cache hit cannot supply a whole-family edge. All four projection directions require scoped feasibility or unresolved-domain evidence. This realizes (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-tile-certificate`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-shared-nodes`) without choosing graph storage, a proof algorithm or a new implementation API.

**Requirement (Indexed input and output bindings)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-binding`

Open capability, owned by bindings and matrix: connect tile-axis input/output and companion symbols to native region-version selections once per admitted family, many-to-many with checked overlap, original source dependencies and total heap coverage. Bind every requested output before kernel execution under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-output-binding`). Supply native containment and version authentication without adding exact literals, per-cell descriptors or admission edges. Equal-valued foreign source nodes, changed maps and widened companion regions must not inherit a tile's authority. This realizes (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-array-binding`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-lifetime`) within the existing binding contracts.

**Requirement (Irrational-root cover evidence)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-irrational-cover`

Open capability, owned by CAS and bindings: retain exact positive-root frequency nodes on positive non-square product domains and attach complete cycle-range and branch evidence using original-target bracket or equality nodes under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-root-brackets`). Share validated brackets and pi proofs wherever domain-inclusion edges permit. Unsettled cuts keep open obligations and complete candidate-domain nodes. Positive nonsquare fixtures, exact-face equalities, zero-excluding reciprocal premises and corrupted bracket/pi evidence delimit acceptance. This replaces rational-root admission in the cover contract without inserting approximate constants into the descriptor graph.

**Requirement (Indexed projection subgraphs)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`

Open capability, owned by bindings for correspondence, scalar for primitive laws and matrix for execution: lower every query in (`sec:nrz:lc-grid-questions`) to an indexed kernel subgraph with all output and companion bindings declared first. Include frequency, sample-axis phase series, both element outputs, correlated alias/time-family projections, members, tails and horizons wherever certified. Numerical edges consume region versions and produce each output selection's enclosure without exact graph growth or CAS invocation per cell. Exact cycle-domain parameters keep admitted bounded numerical readings when arithmetic requires them; floats cannot decide their identity. Unsupported laws leave open obligations. Existing scalar and spectral kernels are ingredients, not evidence of complete family lowerings.

**Requirement (Decision strata and refinement edges)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-region-evidence`

Open capability, owned by CAS, bindings and the decision/plan owner: connect exact partition and boundary nodes with bound output-enclosure inputs to decision nodes that produce checked stratum partitions. Attach whole-family enclosure/cut or existence proofs to yes/no strata, and open obligation edges, last-enclosure bindings and typed stop nodes to undecided strata. Splits add child domains, cover and boundary-ownership edges and cite parent authority without rebuilding it. Support continuous-region and grid-index domains and explicit quantifiers under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-predicate`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-region-decisions`). Native comparisons cannot mint semantic edges, and per-cell proof construction violates the shared-stratum cost contract.

**Requirement (Bounded live graph and dependency retirement)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-bounded-execution`

Open capability, owned by matrix and the plan/batch owner: choose capacity-bounded tile domains, execute and stream indexed bindings, retain resolvable shared authority, and bound the live computational graph and refinement frontier independently of total grid population. Retire dependent output bindings and decision strata through version-dependency edges, preserving immutable historical theorem nodes. Checked cover edges retain refused and undecided subdomains under partial progress. Exported exact graphs and complete materialized outputs have separately measured storage. This realizes (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-streaming`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`) without choosing a scheduler, cache container or wire representation.

**Requirement (Auditable graph cost and semantic evidence)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-acceptance-evidence`

Open capability, owned by the measurement and participating engine owners: supply workload families, independent numerical expectations, exact/native graph counters and uncertainty intervals under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-benchmark`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-counters`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`). Test graph sharing across tile-size and ordering variants, generic roots, exact boundary nodes, source-version invalidation and exhausted-refinement strata. Checked stratum covers must account for every original index and every possible branch; an unimplemented question has open requirements and cannot pass through absent successful outputs. This concept adds no code, tests, dependency, limit, default or literal Work pin.

## Choices requiring the owner · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-owner-choices`

**Open Question (Ratio target multiple)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-policy`

Options are one common multiple of measured uncertified `f64` cost or separate multiples by question/output complexity, with the numerical values chosen after measurement. Recommended position: measure each kind first, then select per-kind multiples as non-blocking engineering targets. Different family and output-axis extents make an unmeasured universal number misleading. The owner supplies the multiples; structural graph acceptance remains (`crit:nrz:lc-grid-structural-acceptance`).

**Open Question (Tile-domain extent policy)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-size-policy`

Options are fixed user extents, automatic adaptive extents, or adaptive child-domain refinement under an explicit resident-memory cap with a deterministic fixed-extent override. Recommended position: the capped adaptive policy with the override, because proof boundaries and error growth vary while bounded live graphs and reproducible comparisons need explicit controls. The owner chooses the exposed policy and cap interface; this concept sets no new default or capacity.

**Open Question (Refinement and publication of output bindings)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-refinement-policy`

Options are subdivision at one format only, a declared precision sequence followed by subdivision, or caller-selected bounded policies; output bindings can publish by completed stratum or after the whole requested domain settles. Recommended position: caller-visible finite policies with explicit error goals and publication of complete bindings over settled or explicitly undecided strata. Open obligations and refusal nodes remain attached to unresolved domains. This keeps physical uncertainty distinct from numerical ambiguity and preserves bounded execution. The owner chooses formats, ceilings and delivery mode; policies cannot drop branches or relax deterministic coverage.

**Open Question (Decision-domain scope and predicate interface)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-decision-policy`

Options are grid-index domains, continuous-region domains or both, with explicit existential/universal predicate nodes. Recommended position: expose both with domain and quantifier mandatory, and yes/no/undecided strata plus distinct domain/refusal nodes. A sampled axis has no implicit continuous-cover edge, and a witness has different force for the two quantifiers. The owner chooses the public interface and any initial subset; a subset leaves other acceptance obligations open.

**Open Question (Acceptance protocol and delivery scope)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-evidence-policy`

Options are one pinned reference environment or several reported environments, and completion of all question graphs together or separately reviewed increments. Recommended position: one pinned single-core release `f64` environment for cost comparison, both supported numerical formats for correctness, and separate increments with full acceptance only after every required query and decision graph has evidence. Choose confidence level, repetitions and exponent-interval informativeness before timing; a 95-percent interval is a proposed convention, not an inferred result. The owner fixes the protocol and first delivery subset so acceptance thresholds do not move after observing graph costs.
